add('A formal Lakeland operator becomes an operator only after its domain is fixed','2024-10-07T03:51:27Z','Functional analysis','Self-adjoint sums and prime-indexed perturbations','L_R=−Δ+λ_R W(z)+Σ_p R_p T_p','Which explicit hypotheses make a prime-indexed perturbation of a Laplacian self-adjoint with compact resolvent?',
'The recovered operator formula is useful as a template but does not define a domain, geometry or convergent prime sum. A rigorous starting point is a Dirichlet Laplacian on a bounded interval together with a norm-convergent bounded perturbation. This proves an actual spectral theorem in a stated model.',
['Let A=−d²/dx² on L²(0,π), with domain H²(0,π)∩H₀¹(0,π).','Assume W is a bounded real multiplication operator, T_p are bounded self-adjoint operators on this same Hilbert space, and Σ_p|R_p| ||T_p||<∞.'],
'A bounded self-adjoint perturbation theorem','For real λ_R,R_p, the operator L=A+λ_R W+Σ_pR_pT_p is self-adjoint on D(A), bounded below and has compact resolvent.',
'The coefficient condition makes the prime sum Cauchy in operator norm; its limit B is bounded and self-adjoint because taking adjoints is norm-continuous. Including λ_RW preserves these properties. The adjoint identity (A+B)*=A*+B* on D(A*) holds for bounded B, so L is self-adjoint on D(A). The form inequality 〈u,Lu〉≥(1−||B||)||u||² follows from the first Dirichlet eigenvalue1. For a sufficiently large negative real z, ||B(A−z)⁻¹||<1; a Neumann series makes I+B(A−z)⁻¹ invertible. The resolvent factorization (L−z)⁻¹=(A−z)⁻¹[I+B(A−z)⁻¹]⁻¹ is compact because the Laplacian resolvent is compact. Compactness then holds at all resolvent points by the resolvent identity.',
'Take W=0, T_p=I and R_p=p⁻².',
'The series is bounded byΣ_(n≥2)n⁻²<∞. Hence B=cI with c=Σ_p p⁻², and eigenvalues are exactly n²+c with the ordinary sine eigenfunctions. The construction yields a valid prime-indexed operator but visibly does not supply a zeta-zero correspondence.',
'Self-adjointness is a domain theorem, not a consequence of real coefficients alone. Compact resolvent gives discrete eigenvalues in this concrete model.',
'The broad receipt labels October7 content U, while the half-month receipt labels the same passage A. Attribution is therefore treated conservatively as assistant-level conflicting evidence. Unbounded Hecke operators or divergent sums are outside this bounded model.',
'Specify the intended manifold, boundary conditions and common invariant core. For unbounded perturbations, verify a relative bound below1 or construct a closed semibounded quadratic form before using a spectral theorem.',refs=('zeros',),note='Conflicting retrieval attribution: broad root_receipts marks U, half-month root_followups marks A at the same timestamp. Conservatively labeled A; no original user wording is asserted.')
add('A Weyl-law obstruction for a bounded-interval RH model','2024-10-05T12:33:58Z','Spectral asymptotics','Counting-function compatibility','Λ_n=γ_n²; Weyl/Riemann–von Mangoldt count','Could a bounded perturbation of a one-dimensional Laplacian have all squared zeta ordinates as its eigenvalues?',
'The historical proposal requires more than real eigenvalues: its counting function must match the number of all zeta zeros, with multiplicity. A basic asymptotic comparison rules out a large class of elementary candidate operators. It does not rule out every possible Hilbert–Pólya construction.',
['For a positive discrete operator L, N_L(Λ) counts eigenvalues at mostΛ, with multiplicity.','The zeta-zero count is N_ζ(T)=T/(2π)log(T/(2π))−T/(2π)+O(logT), counting zeros in the critical strip with positive ordinate.'],
'A concrete density mismatch','If L=−d²/dx²+B on(0,π) with Dirichlet conditions and bounded self-adjoint B, then N_L(Λ)=√Λ+O(1). It cannot equal the multiset {γ_n²} of ordinates of all nontrivial zeta zeros.',
'By the min–max principle, n²−||B||≤λ_n(L)≤n²+||B||. Consequently floor√(Λ−||B||)≤N_L(Λ)≤floor√(Λ+||B||), once the expressions are meaningful. Both endpoints are√Λ+O(1). If an exact multiplicity-preserving correspondence λ_n=γ_n² held for all zeros, the required count would instead be N_ζ(√Λ)=√Λ/(2π)log(√Λ/(2π))−√Λ/(2π)+O(logΛ). Dividing by√Λ, the latter grows like(logΛ)/(4π), while the Laplacian ratio tends to1. Equality is impossible. The argument uses the count of all zeros and makes no RH assumption: even off-line zeros have ordinates and contribute to that count.',
'Compare a diagonal model with eigenvalues n²+c against the required target density.',
'Its firstN levels occupy an energy range of orderN². Inverting the zeta count gives roughly γ_N of orderN/logN, so γ_N² has orderN²/log²N. A constant spectral shift c cannot alter this leading discrepancy. Fitting any fixed initial list would leave the asymptotic contradiction intact.',
'A density comparison can reject a candidate before expensive numerical tuning. A finite match is weaker than an infinite counting theorem.',
'This excludes bounded perturbations on a fixed finite interval, not noncompact geometries, singular potentials, nonlocal operators or other spectral settings. Those need their own Weyl analysis and domain construction.',
'Derive the candidate operator’s counting law independently, including lower-order terms, then compare it with the target zero count before discussing a trace formula.',refs=('zeros',))
add('A determinant identity needs a proved arithmetic correspondence','2024-10-05T12:33:58Z','Spectral theory','Spectral zeta functions, resolvents and determinants','ζ_L(s)=Σλ_n^(−s); Hadamard factorization; resolvent','What exact determinant–resolvent identity can be established, and why does it not by itself identify a spectral determinant with the Riemann xi function?',
'The recovered argument links spectral zeta functions, Hadamard products and zero ordinates. Each object can be legitimate while the claimed equality between them remains unproved. A finite-dimensional calculation isolates what follows automatically from spectral algebra.',
['For a positive Hermitian d×d matrix L with eigenvalues λ_j, define D(t)=det(L+tI) and ζ_L(s)=Σ_jλ_j^(−s).','For infinite spectra, convergence of the series and analytic continuation required for a regularized determinant are additional assertions.'],
'An exact logarithmic derivative','For t>−minλ_j, d(logD(t))/dt=Tr((L+tI)⁻¹)=Σ_j1/(λ_j+t), and logD(0)=−ζ_L′(0).',
'Unitary diagonalization yields D(t)=∏_j(λ_j+t). All factors are positive in the stated interval, so ordinary real differentiation gives the sum of reciprocal factors. The spectral theorem identifies that sum with the resolvent trace. Differentiating λ_j^(−s)=exp(−slogλ_j) at s=0 gives ζ_L′(0)=−Σ_jlogλ_j, which proves the determinant formula. In an infinite-dimensional case, these operations cannot be transferred without checking convergence: for λ_n=n², ζ_L(s)=ζ(2s) converges initially only for Re(s)>1/2, while Σlogλ_n diverges. Analytic continuation may define a regularized determinant, but its value depends on a proved continuation and normalization.',
'Let L=diag(1,4).',
'D(t)=(1+t)(4+t)=t²+5t+4. Its logarithmic derivative is(2t+5)/(t²+5t+4)=1/(1+t)+1/(4+t). Also ζ_L(s)=1+4^(−s), so−ζ_L′(0)=log4=logdetL. Every identity is exact and contains no zeta-zero information.',
'A determinant packages a spectrum that has already been defined. To obtain an arithmetic theorem, one must prove an independent identity with an arithmetic entire function, including normalization and all zeros.',
'Prescribing known ordinates as diagonal entries constructs a model from those numbers; it does not prove that all nontrivial zeros lie on the critical line. Hadamard products also require convergence factors and growth control.',
'Formulate a candidate entire-function identity, compare orders and normalization at a regular point, and prove equality through a trace or functional equation that does not assume the desired zero correspondence.',refs=('zeta','zeros'))
add('A heat trace and partition function have precise convexity identities','2024-10-13T04:34:36Z','Analysis and probability','Heat trace and spectral partition function','Z(β)=Σ_n exp(−βΛ_n)','What can be proved from a defined partition function, without converting statistical resemblance into an arithmetic theorem?',
'The recovered spectral proposal introduces a partition function. In a finite-dimensional model this gives an exact probability distribution and useful derivative identities. Infinite-dimensional heat traces require summability, which is not implied merely by self-adjointness.',
['For real eigenvalues λ_1,…,λ_d and β>0, set Z(β)=Σ_je^(−βλ_j) and p_j=e^(−βλ_j)/Z.','The mean energy is E_β[λ]=Σ_jp_jλ_j and its variance isΣ_jp_j(λ_j−E_β[λ])².'],
'Convexity of the log partition function','d(logZ)/dβ=−E_β[λ] and d²(logZ)/dβ²=Var_β(λ)≥0. For a nonnegative infinite spectrum, the same identities hold wherever the needed weighted sums converge locally.',
'Finite differentiation gives Z′=−Σλ_je^(−βλ_j) and Z″=Σλ_j²e^(−βλ_j). Therefore(logZ)′=Z′/Z=−E_β[λ], and(logZ)″=Z″/Z−(Z′/Z)²=E_β[λ²]−E_β[λ]². The latter equals a sum of nonnegative squared deviations. Local domination justifies the identical calculation for an infinite series if, for example, Σ(1+λ_j²)e^(−β₀λ_j)<∞ for some β₀ below the evaluation interval. A bounded operator on an infinite-dimensional space generally fails this trace condition: infinitely many bounded eigenvalues makeΣe^(−βλ_j) diverge.',
'Take λ_1=0 and λ_2=1.',
'Z=1+e^(−β), E_β[λ]=1/(e^β+1), and Var=e^β/(e^β+1)². At β=log3 the probabilities are3/4 and1/4, the mean is1/4 and the variance is3/16. For a graph Laplacian, a zero mode similarly contributes a constant1 to the heat trace on each connected component.',
'The derivatives measure moments of a specified spectrum. They do not establish that this spectrum is the zeta spectrum.',
'A time-derivative operator with eigenvalues unbounded below does not have a finite positive-temperature heat trace without a different construction. A formal sum Z is not enough to justify differentiation or a path-integral interpretation.',
'For a concrete compact-resolvent operator, prove heat-trace convergence from eigenvalue growth, then derive its small-time asymptotics and compare them with the proposed arithmetic counting law.',refs=('zeros',))
add('A nonlinear eigenproblem is a constrained variational equation','2024-10-13T04:34:36Z','Nonlinear analysis','Cubic spectral equations','L_NL; nonlinear +β|ψ_n|²','What changes when a linear operator is supplemented by a state-dependent cubic term?',
'The recovered nonlinear extension cannot be treated as an ordinary linear spectrum. A finite-dimensional constrained energy gives a complete model: its stationary points solve a nonlinear eigenvalue equation, and the multiplier depends on the state.',
['For real ψ=(x,y) with x²+y²=1, let E(ψ)=(1/2)ψᵀLψ+(g/4)(x⁴+y⁴).','A constrained stationary point satisfies Lψ+g(ψ componentwise cubed)=λψ.'],
'An exact nonlinear minimizer','For L=diag(0,1) and g=2, the global minimum is E=7/16, attained when x²=3/4 and y²=1/4. Its multiplier isλ=3/2.',
'Put u=x², so y²=1−u and0≤u≤1. The energy is(1/2)(1−u)+(1/2)[u²+(1−u)²]=u²−(3/2)u+1=(u−3/4)²+7/16. This gives the exact global minimum and all sign choices of its minimizers. For a nonzero coordinate the stationary equation gives2x²=λ and1+2y²=λ. Substitution of the minimizing squares yieldsλ=3/2 in both equations. The original linear matrix has eigenvalues0 and1, so the nonlinear multiplier is neither a new linear eigenvalue of L nor a value recoverable from the linear spectral theorem alone. Multiplying the equation by ψ givesλ=ψᵀLψ+g(x⁴+y⁴), which also verifies the value.',
'Use the positive representative ψ=(√3/2,1/2).',
'Lψ=(0,1/2); the cubic term is2ψ³=(3√3/4,1/4). Their sum is(3√3/4,3/4)=(3/2)ψ. At the boundary state(1,0), the energy is1/2, strictly above7/16. Thus allowing a mixed state genuinely changes the optimizer.',
'An exact nonlinear variational calculation replaces the undefined idea that a cubic correction simply shifts every linear eigenvalue.',
'The model is real and finite-dimensional. Complex phases, PDE compactness, existence of infinite families and linearization stability require additional analysis. No arithmetic zero correspondence follows from a nonlinear stationary equation.',
'Linearize the constrained gradient at each stationary point and compute the tangent-space Hessian. In a PDE model, state the function space and prove existence before comparing numerical nonlinear modes.',refs=('analysis',))
add('Spectral rigidity is a fitted statistic with a declared unfolding','2024-10-13T04:34:36Z','Statistics and spectral analysis','Dyson–Mehta rigidity','GUE spacing P(s); rigidity Δ3(L)','How can a rigidity statistic be computed exactly on a finite interval, and what information does it retain?',
'The recovered proposal cites spectral rigidity without defining its regression convention. A finite counting staircase and an ordinary least-squares fit give a fully specified statistic. This makes the dependence on interval placement and unfolding explicit.',
['For levels x_j, let N(x)=#{j:x_j≤x}. On[a,a+L], define Δ3=L⁻¹min_(A,B)∫[N(x)−Ax−B]²dx.','Any unfolding map applied before this calculation is part of the statistical model.'],
'An exact one-level example','For a single level at1/2 in the interval[0,1], the best affine fit to its counting staircase is(3/2)x−1/4 and Δ3=1/16.',
'Here N(x)=0 on[0,1/2) and1 on[1/2,1]. The normal equations are ∫(N−Ax−B)dx=0 and∫x(N−Ax−B)dx=0. The necessary moments are∫N=1/2,∫xN=3/8,∫x=1/2 and∫x²=1/3. Thus A/2+B=1/2 and A/3+B/2=3/8, giving A=3/2,B=−1/4. Orthogonal projection gives the minimum squared error ∫N²−A∫xN−B∫N=1/2−9/16+1/8=1/16. Direct integration of the two quadratic pieces gives the same result. Since L=1, no further normalization is needed.',
'Compare with an interval containing no level, and with a change of the horizontal coordinate.',
'If N is constant on the whole interval, choosing A=0 and B=N gives Δ3=0. A nonlinear unfolding changes both level positions and the measure dx, so it can change the value. It is therefore inappropriate to choose an unfolding after seeing which one most closely resembles a target curve.',
'The statistic measures a defined least-squares deviation. Its value is not an operator identity or proof of spectral correspondence.',
'A single interval is not the averaged Dyson–Mehta statistic usually used in large-sample comparisons. Boundary conventions at jump points have measure zero, but interval selection and averaging do matter. Random-matrix expectations require an independently justified ensemble.',
'Predeclare a smooth counting approximation for unfolding and a family of windows, compute exact staircase integrals, and report the window-to-window spread alongside the mean.',refs=('zeros',))
add('Adelic data require a common restricted-product space','2024-10-11T13:27:29Z','Adelic analysis','Restricted products and local operators','L_Advanced=L∞+Σ_p L_ℏp+L_topo+L_coupling+L_time','How can local real and p-adic terms be assembled into a mathematically defined global object?',
'The recovered advanced operator adds terms belonging to different local settings. A sum of operators requires a common space and convergence. The restricted-product definition of adeles and a simple weighted tensor model clarify two distinct constructions that should not be conflated.',
['The adele ring A_Q consists of(x∞,(x_p)_p) with x∞∈R,x_p∈Q_p and x_p∈Z_p for all but finitely many primes.','On an infinite tensor product with a chosen reference vector, let P_k be commuting norm-one projections acting on the kth factor.'],
'A convergent local sum need not have compact resolvent','B=Σ_(k≥1)2^(−k)P_k converges in operator norm and is bounded self-adjoint. In the reference-sector binary tensor model it has infinitely many orthogonal eigenvectors with eigenvalues2^(−k), so it has no compact resolvent.',
'The tail norm is bounded byΣ_(k>N)2^(−k)=2^(−N), proving norm convergence and self-adjointness. Choose each local space C² with reference vector e0, and P_ke1=e1,P_ke0=0. The vector v_k with e1 at sitek and e0 elsewhere is a legal finite excitation. These vectors are orthonormal and Bv_k=2^(−k)v_k. If(B+iI)⁻¹ were compact, its images of this bounded orthonormal sequence would have a convergent subsequence. But the images are(2^(−k)+i)⁻¹v_k, whose norms tend to1 and whose pairwise distances stay bounded away from zero. Compactness is impossible. Norm convergence of local terms therefore supplies a legitimate operator but not a discrete spectrum with eigenvalues tending to infinity.',
'Compare the true adele x with x_p=1/p for every prime against a valid rational diagonal embedding.',
'The all-prime tuple1/p is not an adele, because1/p∉Z_p at every p. In contrast, a rational number a/b belongs to Z_p at all primes not dividing b, so its diagonal embedding is an adele. The restricted condition is structural, not an optional numerical cutoff.',
'Both global space and spectral compactness need proof. Writing a real term beside infinitely many p-adic terms does not provide either.',
'The tensor example is illustrative and is not identified with L²(A_Q). Restricted tensor products also require distinguished local vectors or subspaces and compatible measures. No global trace formula is inferred.',
'Choose a precise adelic representation and common dense core. State local actions there and prove convergence or form bounds for the global sum before studying its trace or spectrum.',refs=('adeles',))
add('Adding time as an operator changes the spectral problem','2024-10-11T13:27:29Z','Operator theory','Periodic time and Floquet spectra','L_time(t)=iℏ∂t+H(t)','What spectrum results from the recovered time-derivative term in the simplest periodic model?',
'The historical advanced sum includes iℏ∂t+H(t). Even when this is self-adjoint, it is an operator on a time-function space, not merely an additional spatial potential. Its basic periodic spectrum is unbounded in both directions.',
['Work on L²(R/TZ)⊗C^d with periodic H¹ domain, letω=2π/T andℏ>0.','Take a constant Hermitian matrix H0 with eigenvectors u_j and eigenvaluesμ_j.'],
'Exact periodic-time spectrum','K=iℏ∂t+H0 is self-adjoint and has eigenvectors e^(inωt)u_j with eigenvaluesμ_j−ℏnω, n∈Z. Its heat traceΣe^(−βλ) diverges for everyβ>0.',
'The periodic Fourier basis diagonalizes i∂t: differentiation of e^(inωt) gives inω times the function, so multiplication by i yields−nω. The self-adjoint diagonal operator has domain characterized by square-summability of n times the Fourier coefficients, which is the periodic H¹ domain. The bounded Hermitian term H0 commutes with this Fourier decomposition and preserves self-adjointness. The displayed tensor products form a complete orthogonal basis. For n→+∞, μ_j−ℏnω→−∞; consequently e^(−β(μ_j−ℏnω)) tends to infinity rather than zero. The positive heat-trace sum cannot converge. Quasienergy is often considered moduloℏω in Floquet theory, but that quotient is a separate spectral interpretation, not a convergent Gibbs trace of K.',
'Let d=1,μ=1/3,ℏ=1,T=2π.',
'The eigenvalues are1/3−n, so n=−1,0,1 give4/3,1/3,−2/3. The same physical quasienergy class has infinitely many translated representatives. Inserting these raw eigenvalues into a positive-temperature partition sum makes it diverge.',
'The time term is mathematically meaningful on a defined domain, but it changes boundedness and multiplicity properties that a spatial heat-trace argument may have relied on.',
'A genuinely time-dependent H(t) leads to a monodromy or propagator problem. The constant example does not prove a general Floquet theorem, and it supplies no arithmetic correspondence.',
'Construct the unitary propagator over one period and compare its eigenphases with K moduloℏω. Keep that unitary spectral problem distinct from a semibounded elliptic operator.',refs=('analysis',))
add('The pointwise product μΛ has the wrong sign for the recovered prime sum','2024-10-20T15:59:22Z','Multiplicative number theory','Prime ideals versus prime powers','J=μ_KΛ_K; Σ μ_K(𝔭^k)Λ_K(𝔭^k)=Σ logN𝔭','What arithmetic function is actually defined by the recovered pointwise product?',
'The recovered equation contains an exact sign and support error. With the standard ideal Möbius and von Mangoldt functions, their pointwise product is negative on prime ideals and zero on all higher prime powers. It is not the von Mangoldt function whose summatory function appears in the usual explicit formula.',
['For nonzero integral ideals, μ_K(𝔞)=0 if a prime square divides𝔞; otherwise it is(−1)^r for a product of r distinct primes.','Λ_K(𝔞)=logN𝔭 when𝔞=𝔭^m,m≥1, and is zero otherwise.'],
'Exact support and sign','J(𝔞)=μ_K(𝔞)Λ_K(𝔞) equals−logN𝔭 at𝔞=𝔭 and0 otherwise. ThusΣ_(N𝔞≤x)J(𝔞)=−θ_K(x), while ψ_K(x)=Σ_(N𝔭^m≤x)logN𝔭 includes positive prime-power contributions.',
'On a prime ideal, μ_K(𝔭)=−1 andΛ_K(𝔭)=logN𝔭. On𝔭^m withm≥2, μ_K is zero. On an ideal containing at least two different prime factors, Λ_K is zero. These cases exhaust the possibilities and prove the formula. The recovered equality to a positive prime-only sum therefore fails already at the first prime ideal. It also conflates θ_K andψ_K, which differ byΣ_(m≥2,N𝔭^m≤x)logN𝔭. The standard logarithmic derivative of an Euler product producesψ_K, because its geometric expansion retains all powers. Multiplying byμ_K pointwise removes precisely those higher-power terms rather than recovering them.',
'Over Q, compare the arithmetic weights for n=2,4,6,8,9 and the summatory functions at10.',
'J(2)=−log2; J(4)=J(6)=J(8)=J(9)=0. The prime-only sum isθ(10)=log2+log3+log5+log7. The von Mangoldt sum isψ(10)=θ(10)+2log2+log3, because4,8 and9 contribute. The J-sum is−θ(10). These identities can be checked symbolically by keeping coefficients of each log prime as integers.',
'The historical J expression has a perfectly definite classical meaning, and that meaning differs from the stated explicit-formula weight.',
'The recovered α-notation for field elements does not resolve the distinction between elements, principal ideals and arbitrary ideals. The entry uses ideals so factorization and norms are unambiguous. No GRH error bound is transferred to an undefined modified L-function.',
'If a positive prime-only weight is intended, define−μ_KΛ_K. If the standard Chebyshev explicit formula is intended, useΛ_K. Derive the corresponding Dirichlet series before assigning any spectral interpretation.',refs=('zeta','ant'),receipt='root_details.json')
