add('A concrete perfectoid field requires completeness and Frobenius surjectivity','2024-10-27T14:58:14Z','p-adic geometry','A characteristic-p perfectoid model','quantum-height-modified perfectoid/prismatic framework','What actual perfectoid object can be exhibited before modifying its structure sheaf?',
'The recovered formula refers to a modified perfectoid structure sheaf, but no underlying ring or valuation is supplied. An explicit characteristic-p field gives a correct base example and makes the roles of nondiscreteness and completeness visible.',
['Fixp and start withK0=⋃_(n≥0)F_p((t^(1/p^n))), witht-adic valuation normalized byv(t)=1. LetK be its completion.','A rank-one valued field of residue characteristicp is perfectoid when it is complete, its valuation is nondiscrete and Frobenius is surjective on its power-bounded ring modulop.'],
'An explicit characteristic-p perfectoid field','The fieldK is perfectoid. Its value group containsp^(−n), so the valuation is nondiscrete, and Frobenius onK is bijective.',
'Every Laurent series overF_p in the variablet^(1/p^n) has a pth root obtained by dividing its exponents byp and taking the unique pth roots of coefficients inF_p. This root lies at the next level, so Frobenius is bijective onK0. Ifx_m is a Cauchy sequence inK0, its rootsy_m satisfy|y_m−y_l|^p=|x_m−x_l| in characteristicp. They are therefore Cauchy and converge inK. Their limit is a pth root of the limit ofx_m, by continuity of multiplication. Frobenius is injective in a field, so it is bijective onK. It preserves the valuation ring in both directions. Sincep=0 inK, the quotient of that ring byp is the ring itself, and the required surjectivity follows. Completeness holds by construction and nondiscreteness follows fromv(t^(1/p^n))=p^(−n)→0.',
'Atp=2, take the element1+t^(1/2).',
'Its square is1+t because the cross term vanishes in characteristic2. Its square root1+t^(1/4) appears at the next level. Arbitrarily small positive valuations are realized byt^(1/2^n), so this is not a discretely valued Laurent-series field.',
'The definition can be verified on an explicit object before any cohomological comparison or height weighting is proposed.',
'A multiplicative tilting correspondence does not identify characteristic0 and characteristicp structure sheaves as rings. Arbitrary exponentials of real-valued heights need not belong to a p-adic coefficient ring or define sheaf restriction maps.',
'For a modification, specify the coefficient ring and the local sections of the intended sheaf, prove restriction compatibility and ring closure, then formulate a comparison map with stated coefficients.',refs=('perfectoid',))
add('A prism is a structured pair, not a new label for a height weight','2024-10-27T14:58:14Z','Arithmetic geometry','Prisms and δ-rings','RΓ_perf,J̃K(X,O)≅RΓ_Δ,J̃K(X,O)','Which explicit prism can be checked, and what data are absent from the recovered modified comparison?',
'The recovered comparison is not a theorem until its two sites, coefficient sheaves and comparison map are defined. The standard crystalline prism(Z_p,(p)) supplies a complete elementary verification of the underlying algebraic axioms.',
['Aδ-ring has a Frobenius liftφ(x)=x^p+pδ(x); on a p-torsion-free ring such a lift determinesδ.','A prism(A,I) hasI defining a Cartier divisor,A derived(p,I)-complete andp∈I+φ(I)A. It is bounded whenA/I has boundedp-power torsion.'],
'The crystalline base prism','OnA=Z_p takeφ=id andδ(x)=(x−x^p)/p. Then(A,(p)) is a bounded prism with quotientF_p.',
'Fermat’s congruence and continuity showx−x^p is divisible byp inZ_p, soδ is well-defined. The identity map is a ring homomorphism whose reduction modulo p equals Frobenius onF_p, becausex^p=x there. Thereforeδ has exactly theδ-ring identities induced by this lift. The ideal(p) is generated by a non-zero-divisor inZ_p and so defines a Cartier divisor. The ring is p-adically complete; because it is p-torsion-free this also supplies the relevant derived completeness in this example. Finallyφ(p)=p, sop lies in(p)+φ((p))A=(p). The quotient isF_p, whosep-torsion is killed byp, establishing boundedness. These checks exhibit all the named data, rather than merely appending a subscript to a cohomology symbol.',
'Takep=3 and evaluateδ on a few ordinary integers embedded inZ_3.',
'δ(2)=(2−8)/3=−2, δ(3)=(3−27)/3=−8 andδ(1)=0. The mapδ is not an ordinary derivation:δ(2·2)=δ(4)=−20, whereas2δ(2)+2δ(2)=−8. Its product rule includes the Frobenius andp terms encoded byφ.',
'The simplest valid prism already requires a precise algebraic structure. A height-weighted version must establish analogous axioms and a genuine site.',
'This proves a base example, not a general comparison theorem. Bhatt–Scholze comparisons involve particular coefficient rings and base changes; they do not assert that any two expressions labeled perfectoid and prismatic are isomorphic.',
'Define the proposed modifiedδ-ring and ideal, verify the prism axioms, and give a map of ringed sites or complexes. Only then ask whether that map is a quasi-isomorphism.',refs=('prism',))
add('A cohomology comparison must survive a coefficient check','2024-10-27T14:58:14Z','Homological algebra','Base change in comparison statements','RΓ_perf,J̃K≅RΓ_Δ,J̃K; period map','What elementary obstruction can refute an undefined comparison before advanced machinery is invoked?',
'The recovered period and comparison maps omit their source and target categories and coefficient rings. An elementary point example shows why these omissions are decisive. Even when both sides have cohomology concentrated in degree0, different coefficient groups need not be isomorphic.',
['A quasi-isomorphism of complexes induces an isomorphism on every cohomology group in the stated coefficient category.','For a complex consisting of a ringR in degree0, its only cohomology isH0=R.'],
'A coefficient obstruction and a valid derived reduction','The degree0 complexesZ_p andF_p are not quasi-isomorphic as complexes of abelian groups. ButZ_p⊗^L_(Z_p)F_p≃F_p, so an explicitly stated base change gives a valid comparison.',
'Every element of the additive groupF_p is killed byp. InZ_p, multiplication byp is injective and1 is not killed byp. Hence their degree0 cohomology groups are not isomorphic, ruling out a quasi-isomorphism. For the derived tensor product overZ_p, the moduleZ_p is free of rank1 over itself, so no higher resolution is needed:Z_p⊗_(Z_p)F_p=F_p concentrated in degree0. As a second caution, tensoringF_p with itself overZ_p is different: the free resolution[Z_p —p→ Z_p] gives after tensoring the complex[F_p —0→ F_p], with nonzeroTor1 as well as degree0. Thus even an apparently harmless coefficient substitution may introduce a higher derived term.',
'Usep=3 and compare the three complexes just described.',
'H0(Z_3)=Z_3 andH0(F_3)=F_3 differ by torsion. The base-changedZ_3 complex has onlyF_3 in degree0. The self-derived tensorF_3⊗^L_(Z_3)F_3 hasF_3 in degrees0 and−1, so replacing derived tensor by ordinary tensor would lose a cohomology group.',
'Coefficient and base-change data are part of a theorem’s content. They cannot be supplied by a shared notation or a claimed period-map analogy.',
'This does not identify either historical symbol with these elementary complexes. It gives a necessary consistency test for any precise formulation. Perfectoid and prismatic comparisons have additional hypotheses beyond this coefficient check.',
'Write the proposed map between explicit complexes over an explicit base ring, state every tensor operation as ordinary or derived, and test it first on a point and on a non-flat coefficient example.',refs=('prism',))
add('Propositional connectives can be specified by complete truth tables','2024-10-13T19:38:24Z','Mathematical logic','Boolean connectives and implication','∧,∨,¬,→,⇔; precedence and grouping','Which exact truth conditions and countermodels replace ambiguous prose combinations?',
'The recovered October13 logic material names the standard connectives. A precise development treats formulas as expressions evaluated under Boolean assignments. This makes equivalence a checkable universal statement and prevents material implication from being confused with causal language.',
['A valuation assigns each propositional variable a value0 or1. Negation sendsp to1−p.','Conjunction is true only when both inputs are true; disjunction is true when at least one is true; material implicationp→q means¬p∨q.'],
'Exact implication and equivalence identities','p→q is false only at(p,q)=(1,0). The biconditionalp⇔q is equivalent to(p→q)∧(q→p), andp→(q→r) is equivalent to(p∧q)→r.',
'Usingp→q=¬p∨q, the biconditional expression is(¬p∨q)∧(¬q∨p). It is true at(0,0) and(1,1), and false at the two unequal assignments, exactly matching equality of truth values. For the nesting identity,p→(q→r)=¬p∨(¬q∨r). By associativity and De Morgan’s law this is¬(p∧q)∨r, which is(p∧q)→r. This does not imply that(p→q)→r is equivalent. Atp=0,q=0,r=0, the first nested formula is true while(p→q)→r is false, because the inner implicationp→q is true. Parentheses therefore change a mathematically testable property.',
'Write the four rows forp→q and the biconditional.',
'For(p,q)=(0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1), the implication values are1,1,0,1 and the biconditional values are1,0,0,1. A short exhaustive verifier evaluates all eight assignments for the three-variable identity, rather than relying on an informal reading of “if.”',
'Truth conditions and grouping provide a complete finite certificate for propositional equivalence.',
'Material implication is a truth-functional connective; it does not assert relevance, time order or causality. Natural-language “or” can have exclusive uses, so translation choices must be stated. This entry does not address quantified domains yet.',
'Build formulas as abstract syntax trees and evaluate them recursively. For a proposed equivalence withn variables, enumerate2^n assignments or provide a symbolic Boolean derivation.',refs=('logic',))
add('Quantifier order and variable binding change the theorem','2024-10-13T19:42:21Z','First-order logic','Quantifiers and scope','∀x(Human(x)→Mortal(x)); ∃x(Human(x)∧Loves(x,Socrates))','How do quantifier order and capture-avoiding substitution affect a formal mathematical statement?',
'The recovered logic examples become rigorous after specifying a domain and interpretation. Quantifier order is not a stylistic choice: it can distinguish a uniform witness from a witness that depends on the input. Variable substitution must also avoid changing which variables are bound.',
['∀x∃yR(x,y) permitsy to depend onx; ∃y∀xR(x,y) requires one witness for allx.','A free occurrence of a variable is not governed by a quantifier for that variable. Substitution replaces free occurrences only, with bound variables renamed if needed.'],
'An exact two-element countermodel','OnD={0,1} withR(x,y) meaningx=y, ∀x∃yR(x,y) is true and∃y∀xR(x,y) is false. Thus the two quantifier orders are not logically equivalent.',
'For eachx choosey=x, proving the first formula. For any fixedy∈D choosex=1−y; thenx≠y, so the universal condition fails and the second formula is false. The reverse implication is valid in every nonempty domain: one uniform witness also serves each individualx. For binding, considerφ(x)=∃y(x≠y). Substituting a free variabley forx cannot produce∃y(y≠y), because that would capture the newly inserted occurrence. First rename the bound variable toz, giving∃z(x≠z), then substitute to obtain∃z(y≠z). The latter retains the intended dependence on the free value ofy.',
'Evaluate the capture example overD={0,1}.',
'The correctly substituted formula∃z(y≠z) is true for either free valuey, by choosing the other element. The captured formula∃y(y≠y) is false. Thus a syntactic substitution mistake can reverse truth even in the smallest nontrivial model.',
'Quantifier scope and binding are part of the mathematical claim, and finite countermodels can expose an invalid rewrite immediately.',
'Empty-domain conventions can affect some quantifier laws, so standard first-order logic usually assumes a nonempty domain. Natural-language subject–verb–object order does not by itself determine quantifier scope.',
'For a formal-language project, assign every relation an arity, every term a type and every quantifier an explicit scope. Implement alpha-renaming and free-variable checks before attempting automated derivations.',refs=('logic',))
add('Exponent towers require a parse tree','2024-10-10T13:05:52Z','Elementary arithmetic and syntax','Exponent associativity','Python formula 2^(3^2)=512','How should a nested exponent be parsed so that a calculation remains unambiguous?',
'The recovered flashcard includes2^(3²)=512. This is an opportunity to make the expression tree explicit: exponentiation is not associative, and programming notation may use a different symbol from ordinary mathematical writing. The mathematical statement is simple but exact.',
['A towera^(b^c) means exponentiateb byc first. The parenthesized expression(a^b)^c is a different binary-operation tree.','For positivea, a^r can be defined as exp(r loga), making the comparison meaningful for real exponents.'],
'An exact associativity comparison','For positivea,(a^b)^c=a^(bc), whereas a^(b^c) generally differs. Ifa≠1, equality holds exactly whenbc=b^c.',
'From the exponential definition,(a^b)^c=exp(c log(exp(b loga)))=exp(bc loga)=a^(bc), sincea>0. For a≠1 the functionr↦a^r is injective, so equality witha^(b^c) is equivalent tobc=b^c. For a=1 all real powers are1. Restrictions are essential when allowing negative bases or complex exponents, because logarithm branches then prevent the same unrestricted argument. A parser records exponentiation as a node with left and right children; choosing right association createsa^(b^c), while left association creates(a^b)^c. No appeal to typographic intuition is needed once the tree is specified.',
'Evaluate the recovered numbersa=2,b=3,c=2.',
'The right-associated tower is2^(3²)=2^9=512. The left-associated value is(2³)²=8²=64=2^6. Their ratio is8. In Python the exponentiation token is**; the token^ denotes bitwise exclusive-or on integers, so source code must use2**(3**2) to express the intended tower. The included verifier uses explicit parentheses.',
'An explicit parse tree prevents a basic arithmetic statement from becoming a notation or code error.',
'This entry concerns exact finite values. Huge towers require modular arithmetic or logarithmic descriptions, and floating-point exponentiation can overflow even when the mathematical expression is well-defined.',
'For a symbolic-language framework, give exponentiation its own grammar rule and store parentheses structurally. Test the rendered expression against the abstract syntax tree rather than relying on a string of caret symbols.',refs=('analysis',),receipt='root_followups.json')
add('The uncertainty inequality is a Cauchy–Schwarz theorem with domain conditions','2024-10-10T13:05:52Z','Functional analysis','Variance and commutators','Heisenberg uncertainty; Schrödinger equation','What exact mathematical inequality underlies the recovered uncertainty flashcard?',
'The recovered list mentions uncertainty without a proof or operator domains. The finite-dimensional version gives a clean theorem, and a Gaussian computation explains the familiar position–momentum case when the required differentiability and decay are available.',
['For a unit vectorψ and HermitianA,B, seta=〈A〉,b=〈B〉 andΔA=||(A−aI)ψ||.','The commutator is[A,B]=AB−BA. In an unbounded settingψ must belong to domains needed for the products or an appropriate form version.'],
'A commutator lower bound','ΔAΔB≥(1/2)|〈ψ,[A,B]ψ〉|. For positionX and momentumP=−iℏd/dx on a suitable Schwartz state, this givesΔXΔP≥ℏ/2.',
'Letu=(A−aI)ψ andv=(B−bI)ψ. Cauchy–Schwarz gives||u||||v||≥|〈u,v〉|≥|Im〈u,v〉|. Hermiticity implies〈ψ,[A,B]ψ〉=2iIm〈u,v〉, proving the bound. For Schwartz functions, integration by parts has no boundary terms and direct differentiation gives[X,P]ψ=iℏψ, yieldingℏ/2. The finite-dimensional commutator cannot equaliℏI because its trace is zero; thus the position–momentum relation is not a finite matrix identity and its domains cannot be ignored.',
'Takeψ(x)=(2πσ²)^(−1/4)exp(−x²/(4σ²)), withσ>0.',
'The squared magnitude is a centered Gaussian of varianceσ², soΔX=σ. Differentiation givesPψ=iℏxψ/(2σ²), with mean momentum0 and varianceℏ²/(4σ²). ThereforeΔP=ℏ/(2σ) and the product is exactlyℏ/2, attaining the lower bound.',
'The inequality is a precise norm-and-commutator statement. Equality is realized by an explicit family rather than asserted by analogy.',
'For unbounded observables, formal commutator algebra can be invalid outside a common domain. The proof does not interpret measurement disturbance or establish a spectral–arithmetic connection.',
'Use the full complex covariance in Cauchy–Schwarz to derive the stronger Schrödinger–Robertson bound, and characterize equality by linear dependence of the centered vectors.',refs=('uncertainty',),receipt='root_followups.json')
add('Least action reduces to a first variation with fixed endpoints','2024-10-10T13:05:52Z','Calculus of variations','Euler–Lagrange equations','least action; F=ma; Schrödinger equation','What rigorous variational calculation replaces a formula-only least-action flashcard?',
'The recovered flashcards include least action and Newton’s law. Their mathematical core is a stationary-action calculation, with an important distinction between stationarity and a genuine minimum. An elementary quadratic action permits both to be checked exactly.',
['For a C² pathx:[0,T]→R with fixed endpoint values, takeS[x]=∫₀ᵀ[(m/2)(x′)²−V(x)]dt, withm>0.','An admissible variationη is smooth and vanishes at0 andT. Stationarity means the derivative ofS[x+εη] atε=0 vanishes for everyη.'],
'First and second variation','Stationarity impliesmx″+V′(x)=0. ForV(x)=kx²/2 and zero-endpoint variations, the second variation is∫[m(η′)²−kη²]dt and is positive whenk<mπ²/T².',
'Differentiate under the integral to getδS=∫[mx′η′−V′(x)η]dt. Integration by parts removes the endpoint term becauseη vanishes there, leaving−∫[mx″+V′(x)]ηdt. The fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations forces the continuous bracket to vanish. For the quadraticV, differentiating a second time gives the stated form exactly. The Dirichlet Poincaré inequality∫η²≤(T/π)²∫(η′)² then bounds it below by[m−kT²/π²]∫(η′)². Strict positivity proves a strict minimum for this quadratic functional under fixed endpoints. Ifk>mπ²/T², takeη=sin(πt/T); the second variation is negative, so a stationary path need not minimize the action.',
'Choosem=1,k=1 andT=π versusT=2π.',
'AtT=π, the variationη=sin t has zero second variation. AtT=2π,η=sin(t/2) gives∫η′²=π/4 and∫η²=π, soδ²S=−3π/4. The same stationary zero path is therefore not an action minimum on the longer interval.',
'“Least” action requires an actual second-variation argument. The Euler–Lagrange equation alone proves stationarity.',
'The argument is one-dimensional with smooth data and fixed endpoints. Constraints, gauge symmetry, weak minimizers and boundary variations require revised function spaces and boundary conditions.',
'For a specified field action, calculate the first variation, include all boundary terms and determine the Hessian on the admissible variation space before making a minimization claim.',refs=('analysis',),receipt='root_followups.json')
add('Entropy is a concave function with an exact uniform maximum','2024-10-10T13:05:52Z','Probability and convex analysis','Finite Shannon entropy','entropy flashcards','Which exact optimization theorem can be proved for the recovered entropy concept?',
'The recovered list names entropy without specifying a probability space or convention. On a finite alphabet, Shannon entropy is a concrete concave function and its maximum has a short complete proof. This supplies mathematical content without importing thermodynamic interpretations.',
['For probabilitiesp_i≥0 withΣp_i=1, defineH(p)=−Σp_i logp_i, with0log0=0 and natural logarithms.','For positive reference probabilitiesq_i, the relative entropy isD(p||q)=Σ_(p_i>0)p_i log(p_i/q_i).'],
'Exact upper bound and equality case','For a distribution onm outcomes,0≤H(p)≤logm. The maximum occurs exactly at the uniform distribution; the minimum0 occurs exactly at point masses.',
'The elementary inequalitylogu≤u−1 gives−log(q_i/p_i)≥1−q_i/p_i forp_i>0. Multiplying byp_i and summing provesD(p||q)≥1−Σ_(p_i>0)q_i≥0. Withuniformq_i=1/m, D(p||q)=logm−H(p), soH≤logm. Equality inlogu≤u−1 requiresu=1 on the support and no missing positiveq mass, forcingp uniform. Nonnegativity follows termwise from−p_i logp_i≥0. A term vanishes only atp_i=0 or1, soH=0 forces a point mass. On the interior, the Hessian ofH isdiag(−1/p_i); restricting to the probability simplex shows strict concavity, consistent with uniqueness of the maximum.',
'Comparep=(1/2,1/4,1/4) with the uniform distribution on three outcomes.',
'H(p)=(1/2)log2+2(1/4)log4=(3/2)log2≈1.03972. The maximum islog3≈1.09861, so the deficit isD(p||uniform)=log3−(3/2)log2≈0.05889. These exact expressions specify the logarithm base; base2 would divide every value bylog2.',
'Entropy is a defined functional whose inequality and equality cases can be proved, rather than a universal synonym for disorder.',
'Continuous differential entropy has different transformation properties and can be negative. No thermodynamic entropy, residual calibration or arithmetic spectral invariant is identified with this finite functional without an explicit map.',
'For a constrained maximum-entropy problem, state the moment constraints and solve its Lagrange equations, then use relative entropy to prove that the candidate is the global maximizer.',refs=('shannon',),receipt='root_followups.json')
add('The differential identities behind Maxwell-type constraints are exact complexes','2024-10-10T13:05:52Z','Vector calculus and discrete geometry','Gradient–curl–divergence identities','Maxwell equations; Ohm’s law V=IR','What pure mathematical identities underlie the recovered field-equation and circuit flashcards?',
'The recovered material mentions Maxwell equations and Ohm’s law. Their common mathematical structure includes incidence operators, conservation and positive quadratic forms. A finite complex supplies an exact version of the differential identitydivcurl=0, with no physical assumptions required.',
['Orient a triangle with vertices1,2,3 and directed edges12,23,31. LetB be its vertex–edge incidence matrix.','The oriented boundary of its single face is the edge vectorc=(1,1,1). For positive edge resistancesR=diag(r_e), use the quadratic formjᵀRj.'],
'Boundary of boundary and a one-cycle energy','Bc=0, the discrete boundary-of-boundary identity. Every divergence-free edge vector isj=tc. Its energy ist²(r_12+r_23+r_31), positive for nonzerot when allresistances are positive.',
'The three incidence columns are(−1,1,0),(0,−1,1),(1,0,−1), whose sum is zero, provingBc=0. IfBj=0, the vertex equations givej_12=j_23=j_31, hencekerB is exactly the line spanned byc. The energy statement follows by substitution into the diagonal quadratic form. For a vertex potentialu, the edge gradient isBᵀu, andcᵀBᵀu=(Bc)ᵀu=0: a gradient has zero circulation around the face. These are discrete counterparts ofcurlgrad=0 anddivcurl=0, both consequences of commuting mixed derivatives in the smooth setting. Positive conductance weights similarly give the symmetric LaplacianBWBᵀ and energyΣ_ew_e(u_head−u_tail)².',
'Take resistances1,2,3 and cycle coefficientt=2.',
'The current vector is(2,2,2), divergence is zero at every vertex and the quadratic energy is4(1+2+3)=24. If potentials areu=(0,1,4), the edge differences are(1,3,−4), whose oriented cycle sum is0. These are exact integer checks.',
'Conservation and compatibility can be expressed as matrix identities before adding dynamics or constitutive laws.',
'The triangle example does not derive the complete Maxwell equations or a circuit model. Topology matters: curl-free fields need not be globally gradients on spaces with nontrivial first cohomology, and a graph without filled faces retains cycle classes.',
'For a chosen cell complex, write every incidence matrix and verify consecutive products vanish. Then distinguish algebraic constraints, constitutive positive weights and evolution equations.',refs=('analysis',),receipt='root_followups.json')
